          OK, here goes. (A bit late; having announced the gesture,
          this site's 'net gateway went mammaries-skyward for three
          days.  Once more...)

          Has anyone out there read much George Bernard Shaw?  Over
          the holidays I picked up _Major Barbara_ for the nth time,
          and was struck by its apparent extropian-ness.  Here we see
          a non-corporate businessman (Undershaft) running, in effect,
          a society of his own, and within its bounds, a relatively
          free one ("--They find their own dreams, but I look after
          the drainage" (III)), and justifying his arms business on
          pretty familiar-sounding grounds:

               ...I will take an order from a good man as cheerfully
               as from a bad one.  If you good people prefer preaching
               and shirking to buying my weapons and fighting the
               rascals, dont blame me.  I can make cannons: I cannot
               make courage and conviction. (III)

          I could go on, but I'll simply recommend the play if you
          haven't read it, rather than sit here and summarize the
          thing.  It's a quick read, maybe a hundred pages as a
          script.

          None of this, mind you, is meant to characterize Shaw as
          some kind of _ur_-extropian; he was a sucker for
          totalitarian memes, for one thing.  But _Major Barbara_
          seems to bear some interesting earmarks.  It's worth noting
          that Heinlein shows some pretty serious Shavian influence,
          especially in his choice of themes (esp. longevity),
          although he doesn't call attention to it the way he does to
          other authors that influenced him, such as Mark Twain.

                                               --Blair Haworth


