Date: Fri, 15 Jan 93 09:48:41 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re: CORP Shareholder Interests

David Jevans writes:
> These books are a nice introduction into
> the literature about corporate America, its failings, and how it must be
> changed in order to remain competitive.

To which Perry responded:
>The notion that "corporate America must be changed" smacks of socialism and
>central planning. Who, precisely, do you think should be dictating these
>"changes" to "make them be competitive"?

Down boy! Down!  David did not advocate socialism, or any form of
government intervention, and people should be free to say "here's a
problem I think we should fix" without folks assuming they are
advocating central planning.   On this list non-socialist solutions
should be the presumed intention, I would think.

David may or may not be confused about a few details, but there is a
wide perception of a short-term focus problem within the business
community, and we should welcome suggested diagnoses and prognoses.

Yes, marginal capital gains taxes should be lowered, but I think the
problem goes deeper than this.

Investors *are* looking for long-term gains, but the problem is what
can they take as a credible signal of such gains.  Imagine a CEO
considering an action which will raise long term gains (say in ten
years) at the expense of short term gains.  If investors are well
informed about this choice, then they should reward the CEO for taking
the long-term option, by immediately bidding up the price of the
stock.  

But in practice investors are not that well informed, and so rely
rather heavily on earnings/dividends to indicate the health and future
potential of the company.  If earnings go down, the CEO may *say* that
is because they've just made a long-term investment, but maybe they
all say that when things get bad.  So CEOs are under pressure to prove
their health by raising current earnings.  I think this is the
standard explanation why dividends are paid *at all*, since investors
would profit more if they just paid off loans or bought back stock.

In Japan a lack of enforcing insider trading laws, plus the heavy
mutual ownership of companies that sell a lot to each other and know a
lot about each other, gives a few investors the incentive and the
information to bid up the stock price to better reflect the company's
long term potential.  Thus CEOs there can afford a longer term focus.

Prognosis?  Repeal insider trading laws, and prefer to invest in
companies where someone has a large well-informed stake.

