Date: Fri, 15 Jan 93 17:11:45 EST
From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)
Subject: Bionomics On Trial

	I just received the tape _Bionomics On Trial_ by Michael Rothschild
from ExI Institute.  I highly recommend it.  It was facinating.

	Michael Rothschild does not consider himself a libertarian, or at
least not what he calls an "ultra-libertarian".  By that, I think he means
a zero-tax laissez-faire capitalist.  I wasn't exactly sure.

	If I may give a rough quote from the tape, he said in
	answer to a question:

	"We can't predict how you make a change in the economy
	that's well meaning, and it literally ricochets through
	this incredibly tangled web of ecological relationships,
	and we don't know where its going to come out; we don't
	know what damage its going to do.

	"That is not to say, however, that 'we do absolutely
	nothing', its a 'no government, kind of ultra-libertarian
	approach', where government has no role.

	"I think, when you get done with it, a bionomic attitude
	is very harmonious with a limited government approach,
	which is what our founding fathers had in mind.  I find
	myself very comfortable with that constitutional,
	limited-government stance -- that there are certain
	kinds of problems -- namely commons problems -- for
	which government is a useful vehicle.  And that's where
	it ought to work, and the rest of the stuff ought to be
	left alone, because it will organize itself and it will
	be much more productive than any interventionist means."


	So, what does everyone think about this?  Does government
have a legitimate role (presumably in handling "commons problems")?
Do we dare trust it with such a role?


Mark Venture

