__________*************************************_____________________

China Really Is on the Move?


Go just about anywhere in Chinaand you're likely to run into a
traffic jam. Swarms of bicycles, cars, trucks, and pedestrians joust
their way around one construction project after another. Skylines
bristle with cranes-so many that you wonder  whether the spindly
gentries shouldn't  be designated the national bird. china has become
the world largest construction site as crews in nearly every conner of
the country rush to reroute roads, bridge unspanned rivers, stack up
office towers, and carve factories out rice paddies in the name of
economic reform.
All this frenetic building is the most palpable sign of the scope,
ambition, and attendant chaos of China's renewed rush to become
market-drive 20 century economy by the turn of 21 century. and it's
not restricted to surban Hong Kong and the ballyhooed coastal
regions of South China.  From  sleepy tea and rice-growning towns
in the countriside to the teeming streets of Shanghai, Wuhan,
tianjing, and Beijing, China is booming.  the only exception s are
remote and backward western provinces, which cover vast area of
desert and mountainous terrain. If  you have been doing most of
your worrying about japan as a tough competitor, you can start
thinking about adding China.
Apparently lost in the dust kicked up by all this activity are the
political misgivings and fears of many ordinary Chinese following
the 1989 pro-democracy clashes that left  student protesters' blood
smeared on TAM Square. While the West can't shake those violent
images, in China what set people's tongues waggling these days
are the doing at the new stock markets in Shanghai and Shenzhen,
the price of lucky phone numbers auctioned off in Beijing, the
"high" salaries offered by new foreign joint ventures  and small
independent business, and other  trappings of what  many would
call capitalism. says  Bank of Tokyo China expert Isao Ohkuba:"
China has already passed the point of no return. The Chinese now
have a taste of how people live in the outside world  and no longer
will they tolerate a lower standard of living. China has to keep
reforming."
The head cheerleader for redoubled economic reform is none other
than 88 Deng Xiaoping. Since early this year, Deng has made a
spectacle of visiting factories and special economic zones that are the
best example of the new, improved  China, and declaring that "to be
rich is glorious." He has relentlessly prodded both ruling
Communist Party and bureaucrat to accelerate reform of state-run
industries and the process of opening to the outside world.
The number are hard even fordiehard socialists to argue against.
The growth of China's gross domestic product, which  has average
8.6% annually since reforms began in 1979, should hit double
digital this year. Unemployment is down to 2.5%, and the exports
grow 16% last year to 72 billion. Average salaries rose more than
10% in 1991 while inflation held 4.2%. Government price controls
now affect less than a third of aviable goods, and more products and
materials are being decontrolled every month. Meanwhile, foreign
investment, which slumped after 1989's political turmoil, last year
climbed to 5 billion.
Nevertheless, China is an economy underconstruction, and
communist foremen are still on the building site, so hard hats are
recommended. Says a awestendiplomat:" China has made all the
easy, win/win reform of freeing up agriculture and small scale
enterprise, setting up  stock market experiment, and encouraging
oversea trading and foreign investment. But  now they must  realize
the state-run industrial  dinosaurs. they must rationalize the Byzantine
banking tax,and currency exchange systems, and they must reduce
both the role and the size of government. each of these reforms face
entrenched opposition either in Communist Party or in particular
ministries  and provinces. I am not sure there's enough political will
do it all."
China's to-do list is as  daunting as it is long. As always,
politics complicate every line item. After  42 yrs of communist rule,
there is still no orderly method of political succession for key leaders
in either the party or the government. some practical matter are even
more pressing. the government  has had budget deficit in 11 of the 12
years since the reform began. Last year the deficit equaled 3.4 % of
gross national product, and this year it could exceed 4%. The
deficit, coupled with double -digital growth rate, could trigger
another round of inflation and wipe out much of the improvement in
standard of living in a matter of months.
Meanwhile, economic reform-along with more cost-conscious
manager- is finally beginning to crack China's vaunted "Iron Rice
bowl" policy of providing credle-to grave job security. So far the
expending economy has created new jobs for those who have been
fired, but  if it stops doing  so, social unrest is almost sure to
follow. reform is also widening the differences in living standards
across the country. Such disparities also could lead to social disorder.
Finally, China must tackle the stodgy, oversubsidized, state-run
enterprises that dominated heavy industry-still mill, mining
concerns, makers of vehicles and machinery. These be hemonths
account for about half the country's industrial  output, but one-third
of them are bleeding buckets of  red inks, and another one-third are
struggling, even with huge cash infusion and price  protection from
Beijing.
All these are structural problems that require stern, painful
change from the top down. But China's answer to many  of the
problems seem to be,"let's build another special economical zone."
Case in point: the government's ambitious plan to revitalize
Shanghai, which China's largest city, with 12 million people, and
the hometo much of the country's heavy industry and its biggest
port. While Shanghai 's stylish  Western architecture has survived
and it's economy has thrived in recent years, the old city , once
know as Paris of East, is simply too crowded and its city services
too decpit to absorb much new growth. So, rather than bulldoze the
Bund, the famous phalanx of art-deco office buildings put up  along
the Huanpu riverfront by foreigners earlier this century, authorities
chose to redevelope Pudong, a  135-square-mile area of light
industry and rice fields directly across the river.
The government plans to spend 3.7 billion there by 1995,
putting up bridges, new thoroughfares, deepwater port facilities,
industrial parks, and China's first free trade zone, where goods
could be imported and exported without duties. Japaness department
and Hong Kong real  estate  developers will put up  thousands of
apartment for sale  to hordes of new residents. "let's build Shanghai
into a foreign-oriented, multinational, and modern cosmopolis,"
crows one of the many temporary billboards at the building sites.
Contral to the strategy to revive state-run industry and
counterbalance Hong Kong's influence after it become part of China
in 1997  is the Shanghai Securities Exchange. A more staid
institution than the wild and woolly bourse in Shenzhen, where
shareholders rioted this summer, the Shanghai exchange wants to be
the home of big domestic industrial companies seeking to tap into
China's vast private savings, now that the mounting deficit is
forcing the government to curtail subsidies.
So far, 29 stocks are listed on the exchange; nine are special
issues called B share that can be bought by foreign investors.
Representatives of 136 member brokers squeeze around 200 person
computer trading terminals, eyes glued to a big electronic screen
where the stage use to be. A new trading room will open this year
with 400  more terminals. While trading is less frantic than on the
shenzhen exchange, the SSE index start at 100 in Dec. 1990 and is
now 800.    (end)
